' THE BANNOCHBURN LEGACY
' From Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books, 1984;
' Interface Publications, 1983), for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Castle Bannochburn: eleven rooms, monsters that come and go (the
' Warlock, the Fearbringer, the Soulthreat, the Kneecrusher, the
' Wolvling), chests, a potion, a scroll and a gilded safe. Find the door
' to the Black Lagoon (its place changes each game) and beat its Guardian
' in three fights, one for each attribute (magic, strength, wisdom),
' ending with at least 10 points in all. Gold buys points before the
' last fights. Every fight is the book's: the difference of the two
' ratings, folded into -5..5, decides how a die roll favours you.
'
' Controls (either joystick): left and right choose (a direction, FIGHT,
' FLEE, an attribute, YES or NO, letters of your name with up and down),
' the button goes on. The second button quits. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - Choices are made with the stick (the book's N, S, E, W, F, FL, Q, Y,
'   N, M, S, W and amounts). Gold buys one point at a time ($100).
' - The listing shows the Soulthreat's words for the Wolvling (monster
'   5); here the Wolvling has its own (lines 350-355 of the book).
' - A harpy takes 1 to 6 strength (the listing's sum can add one); the
'   numbered chests stop at four (the listing's count can pass five).
' - The book's pauses are presses of the button on long texts.
' - Pictures: the castle, the wall, the Black Lagoon, the chest, potion,
'   scroll, safe and gold; each monster has a sprite. New music (The
'   Laird's Lament), sounds; fireworks when you are dubbed a knight.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; at most four
' sprites on a line; every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

